 About: Processes                                                                                   
   PID             Process ID, a number the OS gives each running process. Use it with `taskman     
                   kill <pid>`. IDs are reused after a process exits.                               
   NAME            The program's executable name. Grouping (z) combines processes with the same     
                   name, e.g. "chrome (31)", summing their CPU and memory. The tree (t) nests each  
                   process under the one that started it. ▸ can be expanded with l, ▾ collapsed     
                   with h.                                                                          
   USER            The account the process runs as. Blank usually means a system account you can't  
                   inspect without admin rights.                                                    
   CPU%            Share of total CPU capacity used since the last refresh. Normalized (default):   
                   100% means every core is busy, like Task Manager. Per-core (c): 100% means one   
                   full core, so a busy process can exceed 100%. "--" means no permission to read   
                   it (run as admin / sudo).                                                        
   MEM             Resident memory (the working set on Windows): RAM the process is using right     
                   now, including shared libraries. It reads higher than Task Manager's "private"   
                   figure, and the column can add up to more than your RAM because shared pages     
                   count in every process.                                                          
   Details (Enter) A pane for the selected process: path, command line, parent and children, start  
                   time, threads, handles and live I/O. Hints explain its resource use: ℹ is an     
                   explanation, ⚠ is worth acting on.                                               
   Top line        CPU and MEM as on the Performance tab. DISK is the busiest disk's active time.   
                   NET is total download/upload on the adapters shown. SWAP is memory paged out to  
                   disk; if it keeps growing while MEM is high, you're short of RAM.                
   GPU%            How busy the GPU is for this process: its busiest GPU engine, as in Task         
                   Manager. Watching video counts (video decode). A local LLM showing high CPU% but 
                   ~0 GPU% has fallen back to the CPU, usually because the model didn't fit in      
                   VRAM.                                                                            
